  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong fit: it cleanly separates live-transport from audit-publication, requires durable CRDT milestone citation, and gives peers local ledgers that support long-horizon audit. It is slightly short of perfect because the actual reliable live binding and some milestone conventions remain intentionally unfrozen.",
    "strengths": [
      "Directly addresses the scenario's core boundary problem with layer-scoped conformance claims.",
      "Requires durable audit objects such as snapshots or save-blobs instead of treating a live session as the durable artifact.",
      "Peer-local ledgers preserve failure, replay, authorization, and effect evidence for later human or LLM audit."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Does not define the concrete reliable live CRDT transport, ordering, or reconnect protocol.",
      "Manifest fields and audit-object vocabulary are guide patterns, not yet a frozen interoperable schema."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Provisional live-transport claims could still be misread as interoperability guarantees.",
      "Apps may diverge on snapshot vs save-blob vs op-range-digest conventions without tighter guidance."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What exact milestone object and digest shape should live CRDT apps standardize on at save time?",
      "What minimum manifest verification steps must Bob apply from observable artifacts?",
      "How should authoritative contract-family references work across embodiments and key rotation?",
      "When partition or restart ambiguity exists, when is a break-witness mandatory instead of a receipt?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  },
